you might be using one of the most  powerful language learning tools every  single day  and you don't even know it that tool  netflix  hi my name is luca from lucca  lamprello.com and today i'm going to  show you  how you can use the world famous content  streaming platform netflix  to learn foreign languages nowadays the  offering on netflix  is huge you have all sorts of movies tv  series documentaries you name it  so if you're using netflix to learn a  foreign language what show should you  watch  well my take is that you should always  watch enjoyable content in other words  content that you find interesting and  entertaining  i for one like watching tv series or  documentaries one of the reasons why i  usually watch  these instead of movies is because  they're easier to watch  in short bursts i watch netflix on my  computer which can be distracting so  watching shorter length  content is great for staying focused and  avoiding the urge to browse the internet  or social media movies are usually from  one to two hours in length and with all  that time needed to focus  it's much easier to get distracted okay  let's say you have decided that you like  to watch tv series for language learning  let's say you're learning french great  well then  french tv series it is well not so fast  while watching french tv series might  seem like the most natural choice the  types of shows you should watch  really depend on your level if your  level is b2 or more i definitely go for  an authentic show in that language  if your level is lower you might opt for  something different  instead what about taking a show you've  already seen in your native language  and watching a version dubbed in your  target language  this is useful because being already  familiar with the show's content will  greatly  facilitate your ability to understand it  in another language  and on top of that dubbers tend to speak  very clearly and articulately  the only downside is that dubbed voices  can seem a little bit artificial since  onset  actors don't usually speak like  professional dubbers do however that's a  price you must be willing to pay  if you want to start using netflix  content in your target language  especially if you're not at an advanced  level so  you might want to start watching shows  you already know and which are dubbed  and then moved to more authentic  material when you reach a higher level  the ultimate goal is to watch some  authentic content with authentic  language so always keep that in mind as  you choose  what to watch okay now you've decided  which kinds of shows you want to watch  let's say you chose a tv series a common  question to get is  should i use subtitles and if so which  ones  well first things first i will tell you  that i've had many students confess that  they feel like using subtitles is  like cheating when i hear that my  reaction is always the same  why do you feel that way subtitles are  an amazing tool  to help you understand content and  reinforce  your listening comprehension they should  always be an option for you  if you're learning a foreign language  now on to the second question  should you put these subtitles in your  target language or native language  and once again it depends on your level  if you've learned the language before  you might have noticed that sometimes  having subtitles in your native language  is more beneficial than having them in  your target language this is  practically true when your level is  still not that high  let's say below b1 but once you get more  and more familiar with the language you  might want to use subtitles directly in  your target language and there's a third  possibility  which i'll tell you about in a moment so  keep watching  have you ever heard of the paradox of  choice  it's the paradox that occurs when we've  given a lot of options  prior to making a decision the more  options we have  the harder it is to actually make a  choice  it seems like nonsense but it makes  perfect sense  when you fire up netflix for the first  time and not just the first time  you might be 100 ready to watch  something but you have so many things  you could watch  that after spending ages searching  through the catalog you end up watching  nothing  seriously this happens to me a lot or at  least it did  before i came up with a tip i'm about to  share i would turn on my computer  pick a show stop pick another show go  back to the main page  and then do it all again and pretty soon  i've accomplished nothing  and all i feel ready to do is go to  sleep pretty  unproductive especially when i wanted to  use my watching time to get listening  practice  in one of my languages here's what you  need to do to avoid this trap  decide what you want to watch well in  advance of the time  you actually sit down and watch it  it is that simple in the morning when  your mind is fresh  maybe at work take some five ten minutes  to pick a movie or show to watch that  evening  and once you've decided write it down on  a post-it or add it to your google  calendar  any visual cue or reminder goes and then  stick to the plan you can even open  netflix  and bookmark the url of whatever you're  going to watch so that  when the fateful moment comes you're  ready to get started at a click of your  mouse  problem solved as far as i'm concerned i  know that every friday night if i'm  staying at home  i watch something on netflix so i  already know that  around 9 pm i will watch one tv show of  around 45 minutes  and i've already decided well in advance  what the show is going to be  i've even got my pick for next week  already figured out tv show called  quicksand  a lot of people tend to watch netflix at  night or after work  it's what we do to relax and get the  most enjoyment  out of our free time for that reason we  throw netflix  on when we're sprawled out on our bed or  a couch  and we just lay there and watch whatever  we watch  and that's all good if all you're using  netflix for  is relaxation but if you're using  netflix to learn a language you might  want to employ different watching  strategies depending on the kind of  value you want to get  from your watching time there are  different ways to engage in netflix  watching  ranging from the totally passive to the  extremely proactive  when you watch passively you're just  watching the show letting it wash over  you  just you and your show nothing else when  you watch actively  things are completely different you're  sitting upright  focused pen or pencil in hand and when  you hear a word you want to remember or  look up  you pause the show and you write it down  you're not just watching the show you're  mining it  for new words and sentences to use if  you're wondering which one is best  my answer is both passive immersion is  easier  and requires less effort while proactive  immersion is rewarding and effective but  it's also tougher to do especially after  a long day at work  every time you use netflix for active  watching you should always be in the  habit of  writing down words and sentences to save  for later learning  this helps focus your attention and will  help route those new vocab items into  your episodic memory not only that but  the simple truth is this  the more you focus the more details you  see and the more details you can absorb  the more you will learn there are a  number of cool ways to which you can say  words and sentences for later take for  example a popular chrome extension  called  learning languages with netflix this  extension allows you to organize  on-screen subtitles in various different  arrangements and language combinations  for example target language subtitles  above native language subtitles  not to mention that it has some amazing  features to make the experience of  saving words 10 times  more convenient and fun than it would be  otherwise  please check my video demonstration on  how to use that and you will find all  the information in the description box  as usual on how to watch it with this  nice piece of software you can quickly  save words on the fly  and then review them later i now use  learning languages with netflix  alongside physical notebooks in which i  write down  all the new words and phrases i learned  that reminds me  no matter what strategy you use to save  new vocab  make sure to never ever write down  single words  that's a capital sin in vocab  acquisition single words have no context  and without context  most words are meaningless that being  said  you should also avoid writing down  entire sentences too if you're in the  middle of watching something it's  annoying to have to  hit pause just to write down an entire  sentence  and even if you can save them with a  click of your mouse you might get into  the  bad habit of saving everything which can  ultimately be  a little bit overwhelming a simple  solution is to aim for something in  between  only write down short fragments of  sentences  as you watch a fragment can vary in size  but  should be the smallest portion of a  sentence that is worth remembering for  example if you stumble upon a noun you  don't know you might want to at least  write down which verb it was used with  if it's a verb you might want to add the  accompanying preposition and  or an object there are a few examples  that i have provided in the article  associated with this video so  again don't forget to check it out all  the info is again in the description box  getting exposed to words is fantastic  it's great and saving words on the fly  is  even better but unfortunately those  simple actions  are not enough to commit information to  long-term memory  i suggest that you get into the habit of  reviewing your words and fragments  within one week of your writing them  down  you can do this in a text file on your  computer or in a physical notebook  the means of recording your fragments  does not matter  as long as they are in a location that  is easily accessible for review  personally i love carrying around small  notebooks that i can flip through on the  fly  and review what i've recently learned if  there is one advantage to watching a  movie or tv show  it is that you can see the full scope of  human  communication with pure audio you cannot  tell how a person is using his face or  body  to communicate when you use netflix for  language learning  make sure you pay attention to people's  faces  body language and hand gestures facial  features and cold speed gestures  are part of the fundamental  communication code of each language  if you pay close attention you will see  how certain emotions are conveying  through recurring gestures  actions and reactions hand and arm  movements and so on speaking language is  not just a matter of uttering sounds  it's a way to live and navigate in this  world and you can learn a lot from  observing human beings interact  within the rules of their cultural and  linguistic code so  here's the recap first decide the kind  of material you want to consume  always choose content that is  interesting to you and that works well  with the limits of your attention span  start with dubbed content and then move  gradually towards authentic movies and  tv shows as you gain more experience  always make sure that you have subtitles  at least in your target language  alternate between passive and proactive  listening and when you do the latter  make sure you record or write words for  safe keeping  and keep reviewing them regularly pay  attention to the holistic style of  communication  that unfolds in a show particularly  facial features  and body language so there you have it  you've got everything you need to start  binge watching  target language shows on netflix but  before you go don't forget to subscribe  and check all the extra info i've left  for you down  in the description box if you click the  link to the blog you'll find an in-depth  article with even more resources  as well as a video demonstration on how  to use the language  learning with netflix chrome extension  as always  thanks for watching i'll see you in the  next video  [Music]  you

